<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aretas IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 9 BCE–40 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Aretas IV Philopatris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aretas IV was king of the Nabataeans, whose long reign marked the height of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>'s prosperity and building. His daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a>, tetrarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, who divorced her to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>; Aretas defeated Antipas in a war in 36 CE. He is mentioned by Paul in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aretas-iv.jpg" alt="Aretas IV" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), Inc., www.cngcoins.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_coin_of_Aretas_IV_from_3_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aretas's Nabataean title, Ḥārītaṯ Rāḥem-ʿammeh, means Aretas, lover of his people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> says he was first named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> and took Aretas as his throne name, and an inscription from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> suggests he descended from Malichus I. He came to power abruptly in 9 BCE after the sudden death of Obodas II, without seeking the approval of the Roman Emperor Augustus. Syllaeus, the minister of Obodas, made unfounded accusations against him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; his second mission failed too and ended in his execution in 6–5 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Augustus recognised Aretas only after great hesitation. As the most powerful neighbour of Judea, Aretas often took part in its affairs, and he sent troops to support the Syrian governor Varus against the unrest that followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> in 4 BCE. His court was known for its lack of ceremony: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, recounting an anecdote of Athenodorus, wrote that the king rendered an account of his kingship in the popular assembly. His first wife Huldu, a daughter of Obodas II, appeared on his coins until 16 CE; he later married Shaqilath.</p><p>When his daughter Phasaelis learned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> meant to divorce her to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>, she fled to her father. Aretas invaded the domain of Antipas and defeated his army, helped by soldiers from the lands of Philip the Tetrarch; Antipas escaped only with Roman help. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> sent Lucius Vitellius the Elder, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, against Aretas, but the march was halted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 37 CE when news of the emperor's death arrived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Apostle Paul wrote that he escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in a basket let down through a window in the wall to evade the ethnarch of King Aretas. Whether Aretas then controlled the city is disputed: much evidence speaks against Nabataean control before 37 CE, and some scholars think Paul meant the official of a Nabataean community in Damascus, while others propose a brief annexation after 37 CE. Aretas died in 40 CE and was succeeded by his son Malichus II and his daughter Shaqilath II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aretas IV Philopatris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His divorce of a daughter of the Nabataean king <b>Aretas IV</b> to marry Herodias led to a war he lost in 36.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</a></li></ul></section>
